Aurora's sqlite caches: recover from a damaged file instead of failing every statement

A Dawn cache file damaged by a process killed in the middle of a write (on the Switch, the
game closed with HOME) opened fine, and then every lookup and insert failed with "database
disk image is malformed" for the whole run, so every pipeline missed the cache. Worse, the
error paths returned without resetting the prepared statement, so every later bind failed
with "bind on a busy prepared statement" (SQLITE_MISUSE): ~19 000 sqlite log lines in one
console run.

lib/webgpu/gpu_cache.cpp:
- every lookup, insert and the prune reset their statement and drop its bindings on every
  path (a scope guard), and roll back their transaction;
- an SQLITE_CORRUPT or SQLITE_NOTADB result closes the connection, deletes the file (with any
  -journal/-wal/-shm next to it), creates an empty cache and goes on, with one warning line
  ("GPU cache ... is damaged (...); deleted it (N bytes) and started an empty one"); after two
  such resets in a run the cache is turned off for the rest of it, with one line;
- other errors are logged rate-limited (the first three, then every 1000th, with the count);
- at open, after the schema check, "SELECT count(*) FROM cache" walks the key index (a few
  pages, no values) so a file damaged there is replaced before the first lookup; damage
  elsewhere is caught by the first statement that meets it;
- a cache that cannot be opened because the file is damaged is deleted and created once more
  ("Removed ... and retrying"; was Switch-only in the Switch patch 0006);
- connections are closed after finalizing their statements (sqlite3_close refuses while any
  are left, which kept the file open and leaked the connection).

lib/gfx/pipeline_cache.cpp: the same open retry, and a file found damaged while loading is
deleted and the cache started again from an empty file (seeded from the bundled cache); found
damaged by the writer thread, the file is deleted and the next run starts it again.

lib/sqlite_utils.hpp: is_damaged, reset_on_open_failure, reset_cache_file, ErrorLimiter, and
apply_cache_pragmas (WAL + NORMAL as before; the Switch patch 0006 sets its own journal).

